.TH CLEANER 1 "2026-09-20" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
cleaner \- wraps a real tool for every real kind of bloat rather
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cleaner
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cleaner
.nf
than reimplementing any of them, and picks its package-manager
backend by real detection (`command -v`) instead of hardcoding one -
pacman/apt/dnf/zypper/apk/xbps/portage all real, all checked -
so this actually works across distros, not just this machine's own
Arch-based one. Package cache trimming, orphaned-package listing,
systemd journal vacuuming, container image pruning (podman/docker),
unused Flatpak runtimes, language-toolchain caches (npm/pip/cargo),
stale ~/.cache files, and old ~/.local/share/Trash entries (see this
dir's own `trash` script for how things get there) - each run only
if its own real tool is actually present.

ALWAYS shows what WOULD be removed first (every category real,
nothing fabricated) and asks to confirm once before doing anything
for real - a cleanup script that silently deletes real user data or
packages on the first run is the wrong kind of "real". Orphaned
packages are listed but never auto-removed even after confirming -
a real judgement call (gcc15, say, might be kept on purpose for
cross-compiling) left to the user to remove by hand.
.fi
